INT. BANQUET HALL - QUINCEAÑERA - NIGHT

Crepe-paper arches. A chocolate fountain. A disco ball. Tables set with pink linen, centerpieces of plastic roses. A DJ booth sits abandoned, the music paused mid-song.

XIMENA DELGADILLO-PARK (15, ball gown, tiara slipping, sneakers visible beneath the hem) stands alone on the dance floor, spotlit by a single light.

Behind a pillar, ABUELA CONSUELO (70s, black lace, rosary bracelet) watches her.

Across the room by the buffet, TÍO RAMIRO (50s, shiny suit, tie loosened, wine glass in hand) leans on a table.

XIMENA
Where is he?

Nobody answers.

XIMENA (CONT'D)
Where is my father?

Ramiro sets his glass down. Consuelo steps out from behind the pillar.

CONSUELO
Mija.

XIMENA
He promised.

RAMIRO
He's on his way.

XIMENA
He said that at the church.

RAMIRO
Traffic.

XIMENA
It's a Saturday, Tío.

Silence. The disco ball turns. Little diamonds of light slide over the tables.

CONSUELO
Come. Dance with your grandmother.

XIMENA
I don't want to dance.

CONSUELO
Ximena.

XIMENA
It's my party and he isn't here.

She yanks the tiara off and throws it across the floor. It skitters and spins under a table. Ramiro starts forward; Consuelo raises a hand to stop him. Ximena kicks off one sneaker, then the other. They bounce off the stage.

RAMIRO
Don't do this.

XIMENA
Do what? Embarrass him?

RAMIRO
Embarrass yourself.

XIMENA
Good.

She storms to the banquet table, grabs a tray of cupcakes, and sends it crashing to the floor. The hall freezes. Frosting splatters. A distant guest gasps.

Consuelo and Ramiro both advance on her. She backs against the fountain and the chocolate wobbles in its tiers.

CONSUELO
Mi amor.

XIMENA
Don't call me that.

Ramiro stops. Consuelo keeps coming. She holds out her arms, the rosary jangling.

XIMENA (CONT'D)
(a whisper)
He isn't coming.

CONSUELO
No.

XIMENA
He's never coming.

CONSUELO
No.

The girl crumples into her grandmother's arms. They stand there, a small black-lace shape and a large pink one, rocking. Ramiro takes off his jacket and lays it on Ximena's shoulders.

The DJ booth crackles. The paused song restarts, soft and slow.

RAMIRO
(gruff)
I'll dance with you, mija. I'm a terrible dancer.

Ximena laughs into the lace. She wipes her face. She looks up.

XIMENA
You're the worst dancer I know.

RAMIRO
And yet here I am.

He offers his hand. She takes it. They walk onto the floor in her bare feet and his dress shoes, and Consuelo stands to the side, one hand to her heart.
